# The Fingerprint HAL — One Contract, N Implementations, One Firm Boundary [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

> The HAL's primary purpose is a FIRM BOUNDARY against anomalous behaviour in the R503 and
> its libraries (David, 2026-08-16); vendor-swappability is real and secondary. It is
> device-internal: its single caller is the device firmware, its failures are a closed set,
> and the mock is the canonical reference, the fault injector, and the test double for
> everything above the bus.

---

## Shape

### [CXC-FPHAL-SINGLE-CALLER] The HAL has exactly one caller

- ruling: David, 2026-08-16 — the device owns driver and HAL; the service, editor and station are ordinary bus clients.
- consequence: no HAL method is a bus surface. The cheapest complete enforcement is an import lint: any import of a HAL module in a service, station or editor module is a contract violation.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-TWO-FACES] The runtime face is a plain call; only enrollment pays a ceremony

- runtime-face: capabilities and match — one call, one value, no state to hold.
- management-face: template write and read, delete, empty, occupancy — single round trips, plain methods. Only enrollment is multi-step, because only enrollment has a human in the middle, and its state lives in the firmware's own realization of the ruled message sequence ([CXC-FPHAL-ENROLL-SEQUENCE]).
- resumability: enrollment cannot block the tick loop; each advance takes one step and returns immediately.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-OPERATIONS] The contract — nine operations

- the-nine: capabilities, match, begin_enroll, write_template, read_template, delete_slot, empty, read_occupancy, command_feedback.
- removed-verify-slot: withdrawn by David, 2026-08-17, together with its capability field. Re-adding costs one field and one method; nothing about the part forecloses it.
- removed-roster-stamp-ops: the prior contract carried write_roster_stamp and read_roster_stamp against the sensor's notepad. The set identity (current_template_set_uuid) and the attempt counter now persist in the DEVICE's own NVS per the ruled push contract, so the sensor-notepad operations are removed — see [DD-FPHAL-STAMP-HOME] for the residual sensor-swap hazard this trades.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

```python
class FingerprintHAL(Protocol):
    """One contract, N implementations. Every method may fail with a
    FingerprintHALFailure carrying exactly one of the six closed axes."""

    def capabilities(self) -> Capabilities: ...
    # MANDATORY, total, side-effect-free, no wire traffic, stable for the
    # life of the object. Callable before anything else.

    def match(self) -> MatchResult: ...
    # Runtime face. Capture once, search the resident set, return slot and
    # score and nothing else. No side effect, no feedback.

    def begin_enroll(self, slot: Slot, ordinal: Ordinal) -> EnrollmentSession: ...
    # Management face. slot is required and positional: the sensor's
    # auto-assign mode is untypeable. The DEVICE chooses the slot; it is
    # device-internal mechanism and never arrives from the bus.

    def write_template(self, slot: Slot, template: TemplateBytes) -> None: ...
    # Write opaque bytes into an explicit slot. The push path's only surface.

    def read_template(self, slot: Slot) -> TemplateBytes: ...
    # Capability-gated on supports_readback; fails UNSUPPORTED when absent.
    # Its load-bearing consumer is enrollment HARVEST into the library.

    def delete_slot(self, slot: Slot) -> None: ...
    # Idempotent: deleting an unoccupied slot succeeds (a transcription
    # judgement; the falsifier is EV-04).

    def empty(self) -> None: ...
    # Destroy every resident template. Idempotent. The first half of a load.

    def read_occupancy(self) -> frozenset[Slot]: ...
    # Which slots are filled, never whose template is in one. Used by the
    # device to verify its own load; never crosses the bus.

    def command_feedback(self, state: FeedbackState,
                         progress: EnrollProgress | None = None) -> None: ...
    # Render a COMMANDED semantic state. The only method that writes
    # feedback, and it takes the state as an argument. Fails UNSUPPORTED
    # when feedback_channels is empty, so an unshown state is a known fact.
```

### [CXC-FPHAL-PRIMITIVES] The primitives

- slot: an integer, device-local, zero-based, valid below capabilities slot_count. Device-internal mechanism; never crosses the bus ([CXC-FPID-SLOT-MODEL]).
- score: an integer, vendor-scaled. Ordering is meaningful within one implementation; the value is not portable and is DEVICE-INTERNAL — never emitted on ComposeBus (David, 2026-08-17). Its one consumer is the match threshold.
- ordinal: a position in a person's template list, carrying no anatomical fact and never branched on.
- template-bytes: opaque. The template is the seam — moved, never parsed, and nothing above the HAL looks inside one.
- deliberately-absent: there is no person, character, role, capability or module type anywhere in this contract — [CXC-FPID-SENSOR-HORIZON].
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-MATCH-RESULT] MatchResult carries an outcome, a slot and a score, and no verdict

- outcomes: matched (slot and score present), no_match (a finger was read and matched nothing), no_finger (nothing was presented).
- why-no-finger-exists: a polled interface must distinguish an empty doorway from an unrecognised person, because the second gets reject_silent and the first gets nothing. It is HAL-local and adds no token to either vocabulary above the seam.
- a-non-match-is-a-result: an implementation that raises on no_match has made the normal case an error path.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-CAPABILITIES-SCHEMA] Six required fields, no defaults

- rule: every field is required. An implementation that does not know a value must not omit the key — omission is indistinguishable from a value nobody thought to write, which is the failure the announcement exists to prevent.
- field-slot-count: the device's template capacity; consumed by the push-time capacity validation.
- field-max-templates-per-person: what the sensor can associate — not the game's bound of two, which is configuration.
- field-supports-readback: false means read_template fails UNSUPPORTED, and a sensor so announcing cannot serve as an enrollment station ([CXC-FPID-ENROLLMENT-HARVEST]).
- field-feedback-channels: a set; the EMPTY set is the no-feedback case and needs no member to express it.
- field-captures-per-template: chosen by us under the general instruction set, announced, and echoed as the ceiling of every progress report.
- field-template-size-bytes: sizes the transfer cost; announced because a V1.1-era part uses 768 bytes where a later one uses 1536, and a compiled-in constant would be confidently wrong.
- stability: an implementation returns the same Capabilities for the life of the object; a capability that can change under a caller cannot be bound to.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-FEEDBACK-SURFACE] Twelve semantic states, commanded in, originated nowhere

- the-set: idle, ready, awaiting_finger, capture_accepted, capture_retry, enroll_progress, busy, fault, accept, reject_silent, reject_known, retry.
- the-rule: command_feedback takes a semantic state, never a colour, mode or duration. No other method may produce feedback — not on a match, not on an error, not during enrollment ([CXC-FPID-RING-OWNER]).
- progress-payload: required exactly when the state is enroll_progress — ordinal plus capture number and ceiling — and None otherwise, so the device can render which finger and which press.
- mapping-constraint: reject_silent renders identically to a genuine no_match, binding on the realization table ([CXC-R503-RING-REALIZATION]), while the two states stay distinct here on purpose.
- honest-degradation: with no feedback channel the call fails UNSUPPORTED and must not succeed silently — a silent success is an instrument reporting it showed something it did not show.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

---

## Failures

### [CXC-FPHAL-FAILURE-AXES] Six axes, closed and disjoint

- rule: every failure carries exactly one of six axes; the set is closed and an unclassifiable condition is unknown_code, which is a member.
- axis-transport: the link failed — framing, checksum, wire timeout, silence. sensor_code is None by construction: nothing was heard, so nothing can be quoted.
- axis-sensor-refusal: the part answered with a documented refusal; sensor_code always present.
- axis-capacity: store full, slot out of range, payload too large — the one refusal the caller can act on by pushing less.
- axis-protocol-desync: the stream is phase-shifted; the link is fine and the conversation is not.
- axis-unsupported: capabilities says this implementation cannot do this — what keeps an absent capability honest instead of silent.
- axis-unknown-code: the part answered with a code no revision documents; surfaced, never fatal.
- detail-discipline: the detail string is for a human reading a log; no caller may parse or branch on it, or the free-text failure kind returns.
- disjointness-is-testable: a sensor_code on a transport failure, or a transport axis where a code was received, are both checkable by a test and neither is a judgement call.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-UNKNOWN-CODE-HANDLING] An undocumented code fails the operation and must not kill the session

- rule: a confirmation code the implementation does not recognise is reported as unknown_code with the raw byte. The operation fails, the object remains usable, and the next operation must be able to succeed. Never map it onto the nearest documented code — a guessed meaning is worse than a reported unknown.
- why-it-is-not-hypothetical: 0x28 is produced by the vendor's own worked example and appears in no revision's table ([CXC-R503-CODES]).
- testable-predicate: after an unknown_code failure, a subsequent capabilities call and occupancy read both succeed on the same object — checkable without hardware.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

#### [CXC-FPHAL-SCEN-UNKNOWN-CODE] The next operation survives

- when: an operation fails with unknown_code carrying sensor code 0x28
- then: a subsequent capabilities call and a subsequent occupancy read on the same object both succeed
- part-of: [CXC-FPHAL-UNKNOWN-CODE-HANDLING]

### [CXC-FPHAL-OVERFLOW] An overflowing write fails loud and changes nothing

- rule: a write exceeding announced capacity fails with the capacity axis, occupancy is unchanged by the failed write, and no partial success is ever reported as success ([CXC-FPID-OVERFLOW]).
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

---

## Enrollment and the threshold

### [CXC-FPHAL-ENROLL-SEQUENCE] The normative enrollment surface is a message sequence

- ruling: David, 2026-08-17 — specify the enrollment message sequence; leave the object shape to the firmware. The HAL's single caller is the firmware, so the interface everything else programs against is the bus sequence in [CXC-FPBUS-ENROLL-ON-THE-BUS]: one command in, one retained per-device status out, phases awaiting_finger, capture_accepted, capture_retry, merging, stored, cancelled, failed, and no others.
- terminal-semantics: stored, cancelled and failed end a ceremony; the retained value stays at its terminal phase until the next enroll command. failed carries a six-axis failure; a device timeout terminates as cancelled, because no axis describes a person who walked away (a transcription judgement, one enum value to change if wrong).
- the-firmware-realization-is-private: a stepped session, a poll handle, a tick-loop state machine — any is legal; none crosses any boundary. No session handle or step token ever appears on the bus.
- the-guarantee-that-binds: nothing in the message vocabulary commands the ring. The old compile-time guard (a session type with no feedback method) was a property of one realization; the binding guarantee is now the vocabulary itself.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-MATCH-THRESHOLD] The device-internal confidence floor

- ruling: David, 2026-08-17 — a settable match threshold that must be met before the device emits user_detected. Internal only, never emitted on ComposeBus, settable in the YAML, with null interpreted as unset.
- three-senses-of-internal: nothing above the bus sets it, reads it, or sees the score it filters. A device with a floor and one without are indistinguishable from the bus, which is the point.
- null-semantics: null and an absent key both mean unset — no floor, every matched publishes. Null is not zero and not a default. The same nullable-config shape as the device's location, deliberately.
- not-portable: a floor is calibrated against one implementation's scale and must be re-established after a sensor swap.
- open: whether our floor is additive to the part's own security level or replaces reliance on it — [DD-FPHAL-THRESHOLD-SERIES].
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

---

## The two implementations

### [CXC-FPHAL-MOCK-ROLE] The mock has three jobs

- job-one-canonical-reference: when the two implementations disagree, the mock is the expected value and R503HAL is the actual, until a ruling says otherwise. A symmetric parity statement has no expected value and therefore no assertion.
- job-two-fault-injector: the mock is the only thing that can prove the containment boundary holds — a real R503 cannot be made to emit 0x28 on demand, desync mid-stream on command, or overflow on cue.
- job-three-test-double: ruled back by David, 2026-08-17 — QA of every consumer above the bus needs positive and negative cases without a human fingertip in the loop, even after the R503 is fully landed. You cannot automate a fingertip.
- peer-status-also-holds: the mock is a peer implementation of the contract, not a fake R503. It speaks no wire protocol of any kind; protocol traps are met on the bench by a probe against the real part, never by a serial-level fake.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-MOCK-BEHAVIOUR] The mock

- store: an in-memory dict of slot to bytes, a feedback recorder, and a scripted presentation queue. Deterministic in total — no timing, no randomness, no wall clock.
- construction: every Capabilities field is a constructor argument, so a test can announce any sensor, including one with no readback and no feedback channel.
- fault-arming: declaratively, on the constructor or a script object — never through a method on the contract, which would force R503HAL to implement it.
- the-eight-faults: a capture retry at a named index; a capacity failure with occupancy provably unchanged; a transport timeout with sensor_code None; readback unsupported; no feedback channel; a protocol desync synthesized as a HAL-level failure, never as wire bytes; unknown_code 0x28 with the next operation succeeding; and an unstable readback returning bytes that differ from what was written, so the harvest failure path is exercisable without hardware.
- honest-cost: roughly 250 to 300 lines, most of it the fault model — and trimming the fault model to a smaller estimate would delete the reason the mock exists.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-R503-REALIZATION] R503HAL, every cell a citation

- capabilities: compiled-in constants of the implementation, EXCEPT template_size_bytes and captures_per_template, which are configuration — and the constructor SHOULD refuse a ReadProdInfo model string it does not recognise ([CXC-R503-PART-IDENTITY]).
- match: GetImg then GenChar then Search; a no-finger code maps to no_finger, a no-match code to no_match. Pessimistic budget about 0.9 s, capture-dominated ([CXC-R503-TIMING]).
- enroll: the general instruction set into explicit character buffers, then RegModel, then Store with the explicit slot — never AutoEnroll ([CXC-FPID-ENROLLMENT-PATH]).
- template-transfer: DownChar and UpChar as data-packet bursts terminated on the end-of-data PID ([CXC-R503-DATA-BURSTS]).
- occupancy: ReadIndexTable's paged bitmap decoded to a slot set; the bit order is unverified and a wrong order names the wrong slots plausibly, which is why the parity suite writes a NON-contiguous set.
- feedback: AuraLedConfig through the realization table at [CXC-R503-RING-REALIZATION]; every sub-second state is a host-timed on-off transition because of the five-second animator floor.
- every-operation: drain RX before transmit, parse with a sliding header window, verify every checksum, and treat an unlisted code as unknown_code — the containment invariants applied.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-PARITY-TEST] One suite, both implementations, the mock is the expected value

- shape: a single parameterized suite; no test modified between runs; where the two disagree the mock is expected and R503HAL is actual.
- assert-capabilities: all six fields present and typed on both; two successive calls equal.
- assert-explicit-slot: enrolling into slot 10 on an emptied device leaves occupancy exactly {10}.
- assert-noncontiguous-occupancy: writing slots {0, 3, 7, 19} reads back exactly that set — non-contiguous, because a contiguous set decodes identically under several wrong bit orders.
- assert-readback-roundtrip: written bytes read back byte-identical; skipped WITH A RECORDED REASON when readback is unsupported, never counted as a pass.
- assert-delete-and-empty: deleting one slot clears exactly that slot; empty clears everything; both idempotent.
- assert-empty-store-never-matches: with nothing resident, match returns no_match or no_finger and never matched.
- assert-overflow-changes-nothing: a capacity failure leaves occupancy unchanged.
- assert-unknown-code-nonfatal: per the scenario above.
- assert-no-uncommanded-feedback: across a full match and a full enrollment with no feedback commanded, the recorder is empty.
- assert-no-vendor-branch: the suite's own source contains no vendor token and no isinstance branch, enforced by a lint over the test file.
- negative-control: [CXC-FPHAL-EV-01] — injecting each mock fault must turn the corresponding assertion red, or the suite proves nothing.
- what-it-cannot-do: it says nothing about the ring on real glass, which is a human gate at [CXC-R503-RING-REALIZATION].
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

---

## The boundary

### [CXC-FPHAL-BOUNDARY-RATIONALE] Containment first, de-vendoring second

- ruling: David, 2026-08-16 — the HAL exists first as a firm boundary against anomalous behaviour in the R503 and its libraries; his own vendor experience and the document sweep independently agree.
- the-evidence-is-already-ours: an undocumented code the firmware uses; a timeout stated two ways in one revision; three contradictory baud statements never fixed; a remote stack smash and an unbounded hang in the surveyed drivers — every item cited in C70.
- why-containment-outranks: de-vendoring pays once, at a swap that may never happen; containment pays continuously, from the first byte.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-CONTAINMENT-INVARIANTS] Eight invariants, each tied to a documented defect

- inv-1-no-unbounded-wait: every operation carries a deadline; no loop retries without a counter and a wall-clock bound. Tied to pyfingerprint's escapeless read loop. Tell: any while over a read with no bound.
- inv-2-wire-length-never-sizes-a-buffer: a wire-supplied length is validated against the protocol maximum AND the caller's buffer before any payload read — and the caller's buffer is the binding check, because the protocol maximum is itself self-contradictory. Tied to R503Lib's remote stack smash.
- inv-3-unknown-codes-nonfatal: per [CXC-FPHAL-UNKNOWN-CODE-HANDLING]. Tell: a default branch that raises fatally on an unlisted code.
- inv-4-desync-recovers: drain RX before every transmit AND parse with a sliding header window — both, because either alone is partial and no surveyed driver has both. Tell: a 0x55 special case at init and nowhere else.
- inv-5-every-checksum-verified: no payload is acted on before its checksum verifies; the rule is proven against all 21 worked examples. Tell: any bytes-to-value path with no verify.
- inv-6-no-partial-success: a truncated, aborted or capacity-limited operation fails; it never returns collected partial results and never advances the stored set uuid. Tell: a loop that breaks on error and returns what it gathered.
- inv-7-contradictions-resolve-safe: where the documentation contradicts itself, take the reading that fails later and record the other value in a comment. Tied to the 10 versus 15 second timeout.
- inv-8-no-baud-change-ever: the implementation never issues a baud change; it binds 57600 and leaves it. Tied to the undocumented-recovery brick risk. Tell: the presence of a baud-setting path at all.
- what-tests-these: the mock's fault model, and nothing else can.
- status: draft
- status-note: architect's enumeration on David's containment ruling, open to his dissent item by item.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

---

## Decisions

### [DD-FPHAL-THRESHOLD-SERIES] Our threshold in series with the part's security level

- decision: NOT MADE. The R503 carries its own security-level setting, so our floor sits in series with it.
- proposed-answer: treat ours as the ONLY tuned gate — leave the part's security level at its shipped default, never write it, and do all installation tuning in the YAML floor. One knob, one owner, no cross-vendor coupling; and never writing SetSysPara keeps the implementation inside containment inv-8's spirit. Note the shipped default itself drifted across revisions (5 to 3), which is one more reason not to depend on it.
- status: draft
- escalation: proposed, David rules.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [DD-FPHAL-STAMP-HOME] Where the set identity lives, and the sensor-swap residual

- decision: the uuid and attempt counter persist in the DEVICE's NVS, per the ruled push contract. The sensor-notepad stamp operations are removed from this contract.
- residual-hazard: templates live in the sensor and the uuid lives in the host. Physically moving a sensor module between hosts desyncs them — the host would testify to a set the new sensor does not hold.
- proposed-answer: mirror the uuid into the sensor's notepad once per push and compare at boot; a mismatch downgrades the device to its push-needed heartbeat. One page write per push, no per-match wear, and the desync becomes self-announcing.
- status: draft
- escalation: proposed, David rules; the core contract does not depend on it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

---

## Evidence ledger

### [CXC-FPHAL-EV-INDEX] The ledger — 0 of 4 slots filled

- slots-total: 4
- slots-filled: 0
- status-of-the-layer: no HAL of any kind exists. An unfilled slot carries an explicit EMPTY sentinel.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPHAL]

### [CXC-FPHAL-EV-01] The suite's own negative control

- question: arm each of the eight mock faults in turn and run the parity suite.
- expected: each fault turns its corresponding assertion red.
- discriminates: a suite that stays green under injection proves nothing about anything.
- observed: EMPTY
- status: draft
- part-of: [CXC-FPHAL-EV-INDEX]

### [CXC-FPHAL-EV-02] Unknown-code survival on the real part

- question: after any operation on real hardware returns an unlisted code, run capabilities and occupancy.
- expected: both succeed, per the non-fatal rule.
- discriminates: a wedged link after an unknown code falsifies the recovery design.
- observed: EMPTY
- status: draft
- part-of: [CXC-FPHAL-EV-INDEX]

### [CXC-FPHAL-EV-03] Enrollment step latency on hardware

- question: measure the wall time of each ceremony step on the real part.
- expected: each step returns fast enough not to starve the device's tick loop.
- discriminates: a blocking step forces the firmware realization toward a different state-machine shape.
- observed: EMPTY
- status: draft
- part-of: [CXC-FPHAL-EV-INDEX]

### [CXC-FPHAL-EV-04] Deleting an unoccupied slot on real firmware

- question: issue DeletChar against an empty slot and record the confirmation code.
- expected: success, per the idempotence judgement.
- discriminates: a refusal code means the idempotence is implemented in our layer by swallowing it, and that choice must then be recorded rather than silent.
- observed: EMPTY
- status: draft
- part-of: [CXC-FPHAL-EV-INDEX]

